    on it.
  - Each agent works TDD (test first, red, then green) and commits only its own two files.

  [1mslugify(title):[0m lowercase, NFD-normalize and strip diacritics, non-alphanumerics → [38;5;153m-[39m, collapse runs, trim
  leading/trailing [38;5;153m-[39m. Empty/whitespace input → [38;5;153m''[39m.

  [1mTwo decisions before I dispatch[0m

  [1mThe fork on commits:[0m you asked each subagent to commit its own piece. Two agents committing concurrently in [3mthis one[0m
  worktree share a single git index and [38;5;153mindex.lock[39m — commits can fail transiently, and a plain [38;5;153mgit add -A[39m from one agent
  would sweep up the other's half-written files. Three ways out:

  - [1mPath-scoped commits, same worktree[0m — each agent stages only its own two files and runs [38;5;153mgit commit -- <its paths>[39m.
    Both commits land directly on [38;5;153mexisting-feature[39m in order. Cheap, no merge step; the residual risk is a transient
    [38;5;153mindex.lock[39m collision, which is retryable.
  - [1mSeparate worktrees per agent[0m — true isolation, each commits on its own branch, then I merge both into
    [38;5;153mexisting-feature[39m. Zero race, but adds two branches and a merge for ~40 lines of code.
  - [1mI commit both[0m — agents only write files. Safest, but it's not what you asked for.

  I recommend path-scoped commits: the file sets are disjoint by design, so the only real exposure is lock contention,
  not lost work.
